WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:03.950
- A long time ago,

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.860
humanity Rose to become
the dominant species

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.660
on planet earth.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.520
And we were able to do this
because of one specific trait.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.390
It certainly it wasn't
our physical prowess,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.650
pretty much any animal

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.700
the same size as us would
absolutely destroy us

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.030
in a one-on-one physical altercation.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.530
And it wasn't even our
ability to use tools

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.090
because that's not unique to humans.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.810
We were able to rise to power

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.970
because of a specific development
in the cerebral cortex.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.150
That of abstract thought
the ability to conceptualize

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.960
predict, and plan for the future.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.750
Unlike any other animal human
beings, weren't just concerned

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.890
with what they were going to eat today

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.400
but also what they were
going to eat tomorrow

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.900
and weeks from now and years from now.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.730
It was this instinct
of future preservation,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.820
that allowed us to ration our food.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.380
Start to predict changes in seasons.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.950
We became the only animal in history,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.470
that was able to consciously
forego present pleasure

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.140
in order to pave the
way for a better future.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.710
And while this trait was perhaps

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.680
the single greatest survival
mechanism we ever developed.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.810
I'd also argue it's the very reason,

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.840
why it's so difficult to be happy.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.960
Because the world is modernized,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.440
technology advanced so much
faster than we did as humans.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.600
We live in this super advanced world,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.300
yet we're still packing
around primal hardware.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.940
It's in our very nature to
be constantly fantasizing

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.680
about an ideal future.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.250
We'll work jobs that we
don't like, grind away today.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.150
So that will eventually
achieve prosperity tomorrow.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.520
Maybe if you're a student,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.420
you're grinding through school today,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.790
so you can eventually get the
degree, get a well-paying job.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.470
Maybe find the love of your life.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.530
Human beings are really
good at postulating.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.420
What might be in the future

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.720
and using the present to
prepare for that moment.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.760
And I'm not saying that's a bad thing.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.230
It's an extremely useful thing.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.000
It's a very motivating thing.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.660
We need to have some sort of
goal and direction in our life

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.750
otherwise we're completely
goalless and directionless.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.020
I mean, sometimes it's
kind of weird being human

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.920
because not only do we
exist in the present moment

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.830
and have to do our day to day things

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.100
or duties and responsibilities,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.450
but we also psychologically exist

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.200
at the exact same time
in this abstract future,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.070
in this abstract potential,
that we ourselves generated.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.040
And a lot of the time
it's this abstraction,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.840
that motivates us to
get through our present

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.760
especially if our present is something

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.300
that we don't generally
like interacting with.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.820
But the problem we run into is the fact

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.830
that there is another sensation

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.970
that is universal to
human beings, world wide.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:47.830
And that is the fact

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:51.010
that we only experienced
life in the present moment.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.500
We prepare for a future

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.200
that we will actually
never get to experience.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.150
And if we place too many
cards in the future basket,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.720
at the dramatic expense
of the present moment.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.400
Then our entire lives can end
up looking like a squirrel,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.410
obsessively, hoarding acorns

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:09.020
in preparation for a
winter that never comes.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.650
Which seems in a way like
we're constantly postponing

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:13.970
our own happiness.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.470
And that can be extremely exhausting.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.770
Especially if you've been doing that

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:21.580
for years and years and
perhaps even decades.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.910
It's almost like every single
person who has ever lived

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.490
and who will ever live.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.810
If left unchecked will
hamster wheel themselves

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.140
right into their graves.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.950
So what's the solution to this problem.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.590
I'd be really demoralizing
me coming here and saying,

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.300
you will never achieve your dreams.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.990
Your dreams are just an abstraction.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:39.360
You will never get what you want.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.270
But that's not true.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.030
There is a way to be
optimistic about the future,

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.930
and still have ambition.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.540
Yet still be able to take
some of that future happiness,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:49.820
into the present moment.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.390
And I propose that the
way that that's possible

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:54.070
is through gratefulness.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.650
Now, maybe you've heard this 1000 times.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:59.560
Maybe you've heard that
gratefulness is a really good idea

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.900
and I should just be
grateful for what I have,

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:02.920
but how can I do that?

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.460
If my surroundings are
honestly kind of shitty

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.570
and I understand this feeling completely.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:10.290
It's not easy to be grateful at all.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.670
Especially if you're an ambitious person.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.490
In fact, I would say the
more ambitious you are,

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.380
the harder it is to be grateful.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:19.700
Because being grateful is actually risky.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.830
You risk neutering yourself
of the drive to push forward,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.280
to trend your life in an upward direction.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.520
If you spend your life
dreaming of cookies,

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.180
then gratefulness can often feel

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.360
like you're forcing yourself
to no longer want the cookie,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.770
but you want to want the cookie

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.540
because that increases your
chances of getting the cookie.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:40.730
But I think that's the wrong
way to look at gratefulness.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.830
Gratefulness doesn't stop
you from getting cookies.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.230
It just helps you realize that the cookies

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.620
aren't all in front of you,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.170
they're kind of all around you.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:52.000
And you're more than welcome
to take one out and eat one.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.550
So about a year and a half ago,

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.220
my mom started to develop a
pretty severe case of insomnia.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.170
She's always been a very naturally

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.960
anxious person that kind
of runs in the family.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.820
But we started to go through
some hard times the family,

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.950
and this anxiety really turned into

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.860
a really, really bad case of insomnia.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.670
It wasn't just going on for days,

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.610
but it was weeks and weeks and weeks

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.100
of just barely any sleep at all.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.750
Anyone who's had or severe
case of insomnia will know,

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.040
that you start to become
terrified of the nighttime.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.060
Because you know that
you'll just be sitting there

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.950
with your own terrifying thoughts

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.890
not being able to go to sleep.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:28.440
So this was going on for weeks

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.430
and I was starting to
become extremely concerned

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:32.620
for her mental health.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:35.347
One day she messaged me and said,

00:05:35.347 --> 00:05:38.340
"I finally been able to get
some good night's rest."

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.670
And I was so relieved.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:44.120
And it turns out she didn't
use any heavy pharmaceuticals

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.440
or there were no magic pills involved.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.150
All she did was every single night

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.650
before she put her head on the pillow,

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.500
she listed three things.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:56.480
One, something that
made her smile that day.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.930
Two, something that she's grateful for.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:01.510
And three something that she did well.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.860
She started doing this every single night.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.890
And it's crazy to think,

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:09.420
that just simply reflecting on
the good things in her life.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.970
That her frantically busy and noisy brain,

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:14.560
was able to calm down.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.420
And she was finally able to get the rest

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:18.150
that she so desperately needed.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.060
And she didn't just start
doing this randomly.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:23.440
This is all based on
a study that she read.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.230
I'll put the link in the description.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:26.280
You guys can check it out.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:29.620
I guess what I'm trying
to say is that gratitude.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:30.980
It gets you out of your head.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.410
You stop existing in the what ifs.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.210
And if this happens then that,

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.890
or maybe this bad thing
happened in the past,

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.400
and maybe I can get
this done in the future.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.380
You start to really look to the present,

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.570
and the good things that
are already in here.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:48.420
And I'd argue that by
practicing habitual fullness.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.760
It doesn't stop you from getting
the things that you want.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.450
You still want the things that you want

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.240
that primal instinct is not going away.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.780
But what it does do is
it gives you permission

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:01.480
to pull some of that future happiness,

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.000
into the present moment.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.550
Because if you're never
happy with what you have

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.640
you will never be happy with what you get.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:10.740
Every single night before you
put your head on your pillow,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.460
write three good things.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.070
Something that made you smile,

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.060
something that you're grateful for,

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:17.810
and something that you did well.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:20.250
And this might be
difficult to see at first

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:23.220
and the harder it is to see at first,

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.650
the worst you are at it.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:26.210
But I promise you you'll get better.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:27.750
And as you get better and better

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.080
at identifying the good
things in your life,

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.730
you'll come to realize just how many

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:32.773
of them there are.

